---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CipherDetective from "../../components/guides/CipherDetective";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title="Which cipher is it?">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 02 / INVESTIGATE</p>
    <h1>
      Which cipher
      <br />
      is it<span class="accent">?</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Look for what has changed and what has survived. Use a clue to choose your next experiment.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <a href="#explore">01 / Explore the patterns</a>
      <a href="#cheat-sheet">02 / Quick reference</a>
      <a href="#next">03 / Choose a test</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <p>
        Start with plenty of text if you can. Cipher blocks of five letters are usually display
        spacing, not five-letter words. The tools ignore spaces and punctuation when counting A–Z
        letters.
      </p>
      <div id="explore">
        <CipherDetective client:load />
      </div>
      <h2 id="cheat-sheet">Keep this cheat sheet beside your puzzle</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="cipher-cheatsheet">
          <caption>Clues for common English-language ciphers</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Possible cipher</th>
              <th>Letter frequencies</th>
              <th>Bigrams / trigrams</th>
              <th>Try next</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>Caesar</th>
              <td>English peaks shifted together</td>
              <td>Same groups, all shifted equally</td>
              <td>All 26 shifts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Substitution</th>
              <td>Labels shuffled; ranked peaks often familiar</td>
              <td>Common shapes survive with new labels</td>
              <td>Consistent THE hypothesis</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Transposition</th>
              <td>English-like counts at the actual letters</td>
              <td>Many familiar neighbours disrupted</td>
              <td>Grid widths and reading routes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Vigenère</th>
              <td>Often flatter; one shift or relabelling does not align everything</td>
              <td>Common groups split across several encodings</td>
              <td>Kasiski gaps; average column IC</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h2 id="next">Use two clues, then test an idea</h2>
      <p>
        For a suspected Vigenère cipher, look at letters <em>and</em> groups: if neither matches the
        usual English pattern, multiple alphabets are worth testing. It is not enough to see one
        missing THE. Short texts, unusual vocabulary and mixed ciphers can all mislead you. A
        one-letter Vigenère key is just Caesar; repeated keys can have a shorter true period (MAPMAP
        behaves like MAP).
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Index of Coincidence (IC)</strong> is often near 0.066 for English and 0.0385
        for uniform random letters. Substitution and transposition preserve whole-message IC.
        Repeating-key Vigenère often lowers it. Try a candidate length, take every nth letter, and
        average the streams’ IC values: a length that separates the shifts may bring them back
        towards English. Multiples can peak too. Confirm with readable output.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#frequency`}>
          Test your own message ↗
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}/decks/cipher-analysis/`}>
          Present the original deck →
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        All example passages and ciphertexts are original to this site. The animations are shared
        with our cipher-analysis deck. English group ranks:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.norvig.com/mayzner.html">Peter Norvig’s Google Books analysis</a>{" "}
        (within words; our tools include groups across removed spaces). Vigenère detection and
        statistical methods:{" "}
        <a href="https://cs.uri.edu/cryptography/classical-ciphers/vigenere/cryptanalysis-article.html">
          University of Rhode Island, Vigenère cryptanalysis
        </a>{" "}
        and{" "}
        <a href="https://www.cs.trincoll.edu/cryptography/vigenere.html">
          Trinity College, solving Vigenère
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
